  Bd           0.953 / 0.910 / 0.954            0.68/0.86/0.93     1.10/0.87/0.94     +0.14 (0.34) / +0.01 (0.48) / +0.70 (0.70)   +0.15 (0.37)
(|lr| of yY: B′* 0.07/0.20/0.19, Bd 0.07/0.12/0.13.)
Reading: the measured directional terms carry the gaps of H and K under the move (H 0.48 → 0.86 at
50..99, 0.57 → 0.93 at 100..199; K 1.49 → 0.87, 1.65 → 0.94), mostly through K's term; the y term
moves Σ E[yY] only by 0.02–0.1 but lowers its scatter. They do NOT carry the whole ΣC gap: with K
right in median, ΣC at 100..199 is still overpredicted (lr +0.70, from +1.31), and the 50..99 scatter
stays (|lr| 0.48). So the directional terms are confirmed as the cause of the H and K shortfalls and
of about half (in log) of the 100..199 overprediction.

== 3. What else enters ΣC under the move (H′, exploratory) ==

Level B (and B′, B′*) drop two more first-order directional pieces that level A keeps: K3_s =
κ(B_s,l,l,l) in the T source, and the κ3 coupling −c_s κ3_l/S_c of the T recursion (κ3 = 0 before
the move by symmetry, systematic after it: Δκ3_l = β κ(l,l,l,G)). Hypothesis before looking: adding
the measured K3 and Σκ3 to the T step (variant Bt, and with the measured directional terms Bdt)
removes most of the remaining ΣC overprediction at 100..199 (lr to within ±0.3) and the scatter at
50..99 partly. Also run (exploratory): the candidate decompositions of δK (k_explore; hypothesis:
none of the PBM forms correlates with δK above 0.5) and R = Σ_l E[u uᵀ] against ⟨s²⟩_l P
(r_explore; hypothesis: R_TT ≈ ⟨s²⟩_l P within a factor 2, R_JJ comparable to R_TT, R_TJ small).
Results (H′, `explore` script; medians over inputs, per generation ÷ |window mean|):
 - Bt (measured K3 and Σκ3 in the T step, no directional terms): ΣC lr 25..49 −0.165 (|lr| 0.231),
   50..99 +0.225 (0.277), 100..199 +1.243 (1.243), loss +0.42; Bdt (with the measured directional
   terms as well): 25..49 +0.111 (0.269), 50..99 −0.117 (0.368), 100..199 +0.031 (0.494), loss +0.169
   (0.551). The hypothesis holds for the median at 100..199 only together with the directional terms
   of section 2: K3/κ3 alone fix 25..99, the K term fixes the late overprediction. Remaining: per-input
   scatter at 100..199 (|lr| 0.49) and of the loss.
 - Candidate decompositions of β Σ δK at the measured state (÷ |K|; correlation over inputs):
   25..49: measured +0.015; PBM product −0.010 (cor +0.80), first-order pair +0.004, biallelic
   projection (w-terms + products) −0.010 (cor +0.84); 50..99: measured +0.050; products −0.002/+0.001
   (cor +0.99), biallelic projection +0.003 (cor +0.99). None reproduces the size or sign: hypothesis
   (no correlation above 0.5) false for the correlation, but every candidate's level is wrong.
 - R = Σ_l E[u uᵀ] / Σ P (trait² units): R_TT 1.4–2.0e-3, ⟨s²⟩_l P 1.4–2.2e-3 (ratio 0.94–1.3: the
   biallelic closure of the own-skew part holds); R_JJ 2.0–5.0e-2 (14–35× R_TT); R_TJ −0.7 to −1.6e-3.
   Measured Σ Q^T / Σ P +0.9e-3 to +3.5e-3 after the move, Σ Q^J / Σ P +1.5e-2 (0..24) then −5e-3
   (50..99).

== 4. Why the partner and K terms resist closure (H′, exploratory, single-thread, ~30 CPU-s) ==

 - The partner part J_s = κ(B_s,B,l) is dominated by three-locus third cumulants: the pairwise part
   Σ_{k∈s} s_k D_kl (measured exactly per locus) is −0.42 (H′; D −0.39) of J in 50..99 (10–90%:
   −0.60 to +0.07). So the PBM closures of J, E[J_sJ_t] and Σ_t E[J_s y_t] are not usable; three-locus
   third cumulants κ(k,j,l) would need their own state.
 - The K term: for a biallelic locus (†) gives ΔH_s = β[w_l J_s − 2Y_l u_s + s_l κ(B_s,B,B,l)] exactly
   (u = T + J; a first draft dropped −2Y_lT_s, caught by the Symbolics check (d), corrected here), and
   s_l κ(B_s,B,B,l) = Cov(B_sB², d_l²) − 2Cov(B_s,B)C_l − Var(B)T_s. With a Gaussian background the last
   three cancel exactly (checked in Symbolics). Measured: −β(Var(B)ΣT + 2ΣCov(B_s,B)C) is +1.36|H| per
   generation at 25..49 (+0.91 at 50..99), the measured β ΣδH +0.073|H| (+0.022), and the Gaussian-
   background closure β Σ(⟨w⟩_l J − 2Y u) −0.001|H| (+0.011) (with the corrected formula; −0.032/+0.003
   in 0..24/100..199). The K term is a ~5% residual of a
   cancellation between the background's cubic heterogeneity and the association; closing it needs
   Cov(B_sB², d_l²) as a state (fifth order), with its own source (sixth order) — beyond this study.
 - The selection gradient is small per allele: β·(mutational SD) 0.012 (H′; 10–90% 0.002–0.032) and
   β⟨s²⟩^½ 0.027 at 50..99, so first order in β is adequate per locus; but β²S_c (the lag's share of the
   quadratic tilt (β² − 1/S)/2) is 0.29/0.51/0.79/1.28 (H′) in 0..24/25..49/50..99/100..199: the
   dropped term (β² − 1/S)κ₆/2 on K changes sign late in the move.
 - A first version of the closure that fed the odd states back into the even ones (P → Q → P, H → K →
   T → H) was unstable (development smoke test on one H′ input: Σ E[yY] ×10²⁵⁴); level B‡ therefore
   drives the odd states by a zeroth-order level B′* run alongside (second order in β for the even
   states, no feedback).

== 5. Level B‡ on development (H′, D) ==

B‡ = B′* + (i) K3 = ⟨w⟩_l m in the T source and the group's Σκ3 in its coupling + (ii) the own-skew
y term Q^T (validate/linked_dir_theory.jl). Variants: Bdag (both), BdT ((i) only). Smoke test on two
H′ inputs: predicted Σ Q^T 3–4× measured at 25..99 (19× in one), Σ E[yY] then 0.87–7.6 of measured.
Hypotheses before looking at all inputs: Bdag overpredicts Σ E[yY] under the move (Q^T too large:
its source omits R_TJ ≈ −0.5 R_TT and the stabilising couplings); BdT reproduces Bt's ΣC (25..49,
50..99 within ±0.3, |lr| ≤ 0.4; 100..199 and the loss fail) since K3 = ⟨w⟩m ≈ measured (1.0–1.5×);
neither changes K or H much (the K term is not closed).

